# Nexus Estate API — Agent Harness

This file is the operational rulebook for coding agents working in
`nexus-estate-api`.

It consolidates the enforceable rules from the previous `.agentrules` and
`docs/developer.rules.md` into one execution-oriented contract.

For agent execution, follow this file first. `docs/developer.rules.md` remains
useful as architecture background and rationale. If wording differs, use this
file for the concrete implementation and verification workflow.

---

# 0. How to use this file

Before changing code:

1. Identify the bounded context and named business feature that own the change.
2. Identify whether the task changes:
   - authorization;
   - database schema;
   - business data;
   - API contracts;
   - business workflow/state;
   - cross-feature/module dependencies.
3. Read the matching rule sections below before editing.
4. Inspect neighboring implementation and tests before introducing a new
   pattern.
5. Preserve existing user changes. Never use a destructive broad reset to make
   the branch easier to work with.
6. Before completion, run the required verification gates for the change type.
7. Report every command that could not be run. Do not silently substitute a
   different project, database, repository, or service.

Rule words have their normal RFC-style meaning:

- **MUST / MUST NOT**: merge blocker.
- **SHOULD / SHOULD NOT**: expected default; deviation requires a concrete
  repository-specific reason.
- **MAY**: optional.

---

# 1. Non-negotiable invariants

These rules are intentionally duplicated near the top because missing any one
of them can create architecture, authorization, or deployment regressions.

## ARCH-001 — Feature ownership

Every business file MUST belong to a named feature inside its bounded context.

Valid:

```text
src/modules/provider/account/services/...
src/modules/provider/authorization/services/...
src/modules/estate/property/repositories/...
```

Invalid:

```text
src/modules/provider/services/...
src/modules/provider/repositories/...
src/modules/estate/controllers/...
```

The module root is a composition root, not a business implementation layer.

## AUTH-001 — Realm separation

Authentication establishes identity. Platform context establishes the domain.
Authority is valid only inside the platform that owns it.

Customer, Provider, and Administration are separate authorization realms.

MUST NOT:

- use a Customer token as Administration authority;
- use an Administration token as Customer authority;
- treat a global role name as proof of Provider capability;
- reintroduce `ROLES.CUSTOMER`, `ROLES.PROVIDER`, or
  `ROLES.ADMINISTRATOR` as platform identity semantics.

## AUTH-002 — Provider supply ownership

Provider is the canonical owner of supply.

```text
CustomerAccount = authenticated actor / Marketplace identity
ProviderAccount = canonical supply-side business identity
ProviderMembership = Customer access to a Provider
Estate.providerId = canonical Property ownership
Estate.customerId = compatibility/provenance only
```

Provider supply authorization MUST resolve Provider context and compare the
supply record's Provider ownership.

Forbidden as Provider supply authorization:

```ts
estate.customerId === currentCustomerId
```

Required direction:

```ts
const context = await providerContextResolver.resolve(...);

estate.providerId === context.providerId
```

Do not manually thread Provider IDs through every application layer when the
Provider context boundary already exists.

## MIG-001 — Migration timestamp

Before creating a new TypeORM migration, MUST run:

```bash
date +%s%3N
```

Use the exact returned 13-digit Unix-millisecond value as the migration
identity.

Required:

```text
<timestamp>-<Description>.ts
```

and:

```ts
export class <Description><timestamp> implements MigrationInterface
```

MUST NOT:

- invent a timestamp;
- round a timestamp;
- use a memorable date/time;
- derive one from a release date;
- copy another migration timestamp and increment it manually;
- use relative numbers such as `001`, `002`;
- use directory ordering as migration ordering.

Example of an invalid manually chosen identity:

```text
1789584000000-MyMigration.ts
```

The migration timestamp represents the actual creation command, not an
approximate business date.

Before review, MUST run:

```bash
npm run migration:check-timestamps
npm run migration:verify
```

## MIG-002 — Migration ownership

Every schema migration MUST live under the concrete feature that owns the
resulting schema contract:

```text
src/modules/<module>/<feature>/migrations/
```

`src/database/migrations/` is not a valid location for new schema migrations.

A cross-module change still needs one explicit owning feature.

## TEST-001 — Test behavior, not file existence

Tests are required according to behavior and risk.

There is **no repository-wide rule that every production TypeScript file must
have a colocated sidecar test**.

Do not create trivial tests such as:

```text
"module loads"
"export exists"
"file can be imported"
```

solely to satisfy a filename pairing convention.

Behavior-bearing services, policies, guards, strategies, repositories with
custom queries, migration helpers, authorization evaluators, mappers, and
critical controllers normally require focused behavioral coverage.

Pure interfaces/types, simple DTO declarations, enums, constants, barrels,
simple entities, and wiring-only files may be covered indirectly when they
contain no meaningful behavior.

## SAFE-001 — Project isolation

Do not inspect, use, modify, connect to, or borrow infrastructure from any
Jobtik repository, service, container, database, or worktree for this project.

Use only Nexus Estate resources configured for this repository.

---

# 2. Architecture and source layout

Nexus Estate API is a modular NestJS application.

A module is a bounded context. A feature is a business capability or lifecycle
boundary inside that context.

Required shape:

```text
src/modules/<module>/
├── <module>.module.ts
├── <module>.module.spec.ts        # when behavior warrants it
├── <feature-a>/
│   ├── controllers/
│   ├── services/
│   ├── repositories/
│   ├── entities/
│   ├── dto/
│   ├── guards/
│   ├── strategies/
│   ├── helpers/
│   ├── errors/
│   ├── constants/
│   ├── types/
│   ├── migrations/
│   └── data-migrations/
└── <feature-b>/
```

Only create directories that are relevant to the feature.

The feature root may contain feature composition files and feature-owned layer
or lifecycle directories. Do not flatten unrelated production code into the
feature root.

## 2.1 Current feature ownership map

| Module | Feature | Responsibility |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `customer` | `account` | Customer profile, registration, persistence |
| `customer` | `authentication` | Public login, JWT, Customer realm guards |
| `customer` | `authorization` | Marketplace authorization data |
| `provider` | `account` | ProviderAccount lifecycle and Provider context |
| `provider` | `registration` | Provider onboarding and compatibility backfills |
| `provider` | `authorization` | ProviderMembership, Provider roles/permissions |
| `administration` | `authentication` | Internal Administration realm |
| `administration` | `provider-review` | Provider approval workflow |
| `administration` | `authorization` | Administration RBAC, management, audit |
| `estate` | `property` | Property/Estate supply persistence and behavior |
| `location` | `administrative-division` | Province/Ward/address data |
| `lead` | `lead` | Lead persistence and behavior |
| `media` | `asset` | Media persistence and behavior |
| `rbac` | `legacy-global` | Historical compatibility only |

## 2.2 Module root rule

Direct children of `src/modules/<module>/` MUST be named features or module
composition files.

MUST NOT create module-root technical layers such as:

```text
controllers/
services/
repositories/
models/
entities/
dto/
guards/
strategies/
helpers/
enums/
types/
errors/
constants/
permissions/
```

## 2.3 Cross-cutting directories

Use these only for genuinely cross-cutting concerns:

- `src/common/`: shared NestJS/cross-cutting behavior.
- `src/services/abstraction-services/`: shared persistence/service
  primitives.
- `src/utils/`: domain-neutral utilities only.
- `src/database/`: database bootstrap and infrastructure-owned seed adapters.

Do not move domain behavior to a shared directory merely to avoid choosing an
owner.

---

# 3. Dependency and composition rules

## DEP-001 — Feature contracts

A feature may consume another feature through its public contract.

Do not reach into another feature's private implementation if an exported
service/type/contract exists.

A barrel may expose a feature's public contract. It MUST NOT be used to hide
cross-feature coupling or recreate a global namespace.

## DEP-002 — Forbidden authorization imports

- Customer code MUST NOT import Administration authorization.
- Provider code MUST NOT import Administration authorization.
- Estate/property MUST NOT depend on legacy `RoleService`, `RoleGuard`,
  `PermissionsGuard`, or `ROLES.PROVIDER`.
- New Customer, Provider, Administration, or Property runtime code MUST NOT
  depend on `rbac/legacy-global`.

`rbac/legacy-global` is allowed only for historical migrations, rollback
compatibility, explicitly quarantined legacy code, or legacy tests.

## DEP-003 — Circular dependencies

Do not create circular feature/module dependencies.

If a cycle appears:

1. identify the true owner of the shared contract;
2. move the contract to that feature's public API; or
3. use a small domain-neutral shared contract only when no feature owns it.

Do not hide cycles behind barrels or forwarding wrappers.

## DEP-004 — Avoid empty abstraction layers

Do not add a service, resolver, facade, policy, mapper, or wrapper that only
passes arguments through without owning behavior, policy, translation, or a
stable boundary.

Prefer the simplest layer that preserves the domain invariant.

---

# 4. Identity and authorization rules

## 4.1 Realm-specific principals

Customer principal:

```ts
type CustomerPrincipal = {
  id: string;
  email: string;
  realm: 'customer';
};
```

Administration principal:

```ts
type AdministrationPrincipal = {
  id: string;
  email: string;
  realm: 'administration';
};
```

JWTs carry identity and realm context. Do not treat them as long-lived snapshots
of current permissions, Provider state, or Administration authority.

Current database state remains authoritative.

## 4.2 Marketplace

Basic Customer behavior uses authentication, resource ownership, and business
policy.

Do not invent a default `CUSTOMER` RBAC role merely to represent login.

## 4.3 Provider

Provider capability comes from current:

- Provider context;
- ProviderMembership;
- membership status;
- ProviderAccount status;
- verification status;
- permissions/roles where the feature requires them;
- resource ownership;
- workflow/state invariant.

Provider supply mutation requires at minimum:

```text
membership ACTIVE
ProviderAccount ACTIVE
verification VERIFIED
resource owned by resolved Provider
```

Use `ProviderContextResolver` for context selection.

The server is authoritative. A client-supplied `X-Provider-Id` selects a
candidate context; it never grants authority by itself.

## 4.4 Administration

Administration endpoint access requires:

- valid Administration authentication;
- active Administrator state;
- current Administration permission from current DB assignments.

Permission assignment/removal MUST take effect on subsequent requests. Do not
cache durable permission snapshots inside JWTs.

Prefer permission checks over role-name checks.

A role such as `SUPER_ADMIN` MUST NOT replace a specific permission check
unless role identity itself is the business requirement.

## 4.5 Future abstractions

Do not prematurely introduce:

- generic `AuthIdentity`;
- universal principal;
- generic policy engine;
- shared cross-platform role table;
- generic role engine.

Future architecture possibilities are not justification for abstractions before
a real use case exists.

---

# 5. Database and migration rules

## DB-001 — Ownership and naming

Entities and repositories belong to the feature that owns their persistence
contract.

Database naming:

```text
table: tbl_*
column: snake_case
```

Use the repository's approved base entity where appropriate.

## MIG-003 — Append-only shared history

Once a migration has reached a shared or deployed database, MUST NOT edit,
rename, reorder, or reuse its identity.

Add a new migration instead.

A migration still only on an unshared feature branch may be corrected before
merge, but all filename, class-name, test, import, documentation, and discovery
references MUST be updated together.

## MIG-004 — Schema migration vs data migration

Schema migrations change persistence structure and live in:

```text
<feature>/migrations/
```

Business-data backfills live in:

```text
<feature>/data-migrations/
```

Data migrations SHOULD be idempotent when operationally practical and MUST have
integration coverage for important invariants.

Reference/bootstrap data belongs in database seed infrastructure.

Do not use application startup as an implicit data-migration mechanism.

## MIG-005 — Destructive change workflow

Use:

```text
expand -> backfill/cutover -> verify -> contract -> cleanup
```

Do not drop legacy columns/tables while an older application version may still
be running.

Contract migrations MUST verify prerequisites before making a destructive
constraint change.

## MIG-006 — Deterministic migrations

Every migration MUST:

- have deterministic `up()`;
- have a deliberate `down()` appropriate to the migration's rollback
  contract;
- use TypeORM `QueryRunner` and migration-local SQL/metadata;
- avoid importing application services;
- preserve valid existing lifecycle/authorization state unless the migration's
  explicit business purpose is to change it.

For multi-table business mutations outside migrations, use a transaction so
business changes and audit records commit atomically.

## MIG-007 — Global ordering

Folder hierarchy does not determine execution order.

The 13-digit timestamp in the migration identity determines global ordering.

Migration timestamps MUST be unique. Do not add an allowlist entry for a new
collision merely to make CI pass.

## MIG-008 — Mandatory migration checks

For any schema migration change, run:

```bash
npm run migration:check-timestamps
npm run migration:verify
```

Also run the relevant migration/integration suite.

If the configured Nexus Estate PostgreSQL environment or Docker requirement is
unavailable, report it explicitly. Do not substitute another project's DB.

---

# 6. DTO, API, and error rules

## API-001 — Validate at HTTP boundaries

DTOs MUST validate applicable:

- UUIDs;
- enums;
- string lengths;
- numeric bounds;
- array bounds;
- uniqueness;
- whitelisted sort/filter fields.

Never accept raw SQL fragments for filtering or sorting.

## API-002 — Explicit response contracts

Keep response DTOs feature-specific and document them in Swagger.

Do not expose persistence entities as accidental public contracts when the
entity contains internal fields or legacy compatibility state.

Do not expose:

- passwords;
- tokens;
- unnecessary PII;
- internal ownership fields not part of the wire contract;
- internal audit metadata unless intentionally part of the API contract.

## API-003 — Ownership inputs

Ownership fields derived from authenticated identity or Provider context MUST
NOT be accepted from the request body merely for convenience.

For Provider supply, clients may select Provider context through the supported
context mechanism, but the server resolves and validates ownership.

## ERR-001 — Stable business errors

Business failures use the repository's `BusinessException` and stable,
feature-owned error codes.

Every business error definition MUST expose:

```ts
messages: {
  en: ...,
  vi: ...,
}
```

Values MUST come from the feature's message catalogue, normally
`messages.json`.

Do not silently use one language as the fallback value for both languages.

---

# 7. Testing rules

## TEST-002 — Focus on risk and behavior

Normally add direct focused tests for:

- services with business decisions;
- policies;
- guards;
- strategies;
- repositories with custom queries;
- authorization evaluators;
- migration helpers;
- logic-bearing mappers;
- middleware/interceptors with behavior;
- regression bugs.

Coverage should include the relevant combination of:

- happy path;
- invalid input;
- denied/forbidden behavior;
- ownership/scope boundaries;
- lifecycle/state failures;
- duplicate/idempotency behavior;
- rollback/atomicity;
- concurrency/revocation when relevant.

Never weaken an assertion merely to make CI pass.

## TEST-003 — Full-flow business tests

Every user-visible business workflow or critical business feature SHOULD have
full-flow coverage under:

```text
test/<module>/<feature>.spec.ts
```

Exercise the real boundary appropriate to the feature:

```text
HTTP/module
  -> guard
  -> controller
  -> service/policy
  -> repository
  -> database
```

Unit mocks do not replace full-flow coverage for critical behavior.

## TEST-004 — Integration/migration location

Existing infrastructure and migration suites may live under:

```text
test/integration/
```

Do not create a second top-level `tests/` directory.

## TEST-005 — Bug regression

Every fixed bug MUST get a regression test at the narrowest level that would
have caught the bug, plus full-flow coverage when the bug affected an external
workflow.

Examples:

- authorization bug -> policy/service/hook equivalent plus endpoint flow;
- migration bug -> migration integration test;
- ownership leak -> cross-provider full-flow test;
- response-shape bug -> HTTP contract test.

---

# 8. TypeScript, imports, and naming

## TS-001 — Type safety

Avoid:

- `any`;
- `@ts-ignore`;
- unchecked boundary values.

Use `import type` for type-only imports.

## TS-002 — Import direction

Prefer feature-owned public contracts where available.

Do not import stale module-wide layer paths such as:

```text
../services/...
../models/...
../controllers/...
```

when the code now belongs to a named feature.

## TS-003 — Naming

Use explicit business names:

```text
CustomerAccountService
ProviderAuthorizationService
ProviderContextResolver
AdministrationPermissionsGuard
```

Avoid ambiguous production filenames such as:

```text
service.ts
entity.ts
common.dto.ts
utils.ts
```

unless the file is intentionally scoped and unambiguous inside its feature.

A feature-local `index.ts` is allowed only as a deliberate public barrel.

---

# 9. Documentation and comments

Documentation explains intent, invariant, side effect, boundary, or safe use.
It should not restate syntax.

Public behavior-bearing classes SHOULD explain:

- what responsibility they own;
- why callers use them;
- important authorization or persistence boundaries;
- relevant side effects/invariants.

Public methods in services, controllers, repositories, guards, strategies,
policies, middleware/interceptors, migration helpers, and evaluators SHOULD
document non-obvious behavior.

Use `@param`, `@returns`, and `@throws` when they add information rather
than duplicate TypeScript.

Private helpers need comments when their business meaning or safety constraint
is not obvious from the name/signature.

Keep comments truthful when behavior changes.

---

# 10. Change-specific workflows

## 10.1 Schema migration workflow

Before editing:

1. identify the owning feature;
2. inspect neighboring migrations;
3. run `date +%s%3N`;
4. use that exact timestamp;
5. decide whether the change is schema migration or business-data migration;
6. define rollback behavior;
7. define migration integration tests.

Before completion:

```bash
npm run migration:check-timestamps
npm run migration:verify
```

plus relevant integration tests.

Review checklist:

- [ ] timestamp came from `date +%s%3N`;
- [ ] filename/class timestamp match exactly;
- [ ] timestamp is globally unique;
- [ ] migration lives under the owning feature;
- [ ] no application service imports;
- [ ] `up()` and `down()` are deliberate;
- [ ] expand/backfill/verify/contract ordering is safe;
- [ ] old deployed migration history was not rewritten;
- [ ] regression/integration coverage exists.

## 10.2 Authorization workflow

Before editing, write down:

```text
principal
platform
resolved context
resource owner
required lifecycle state
required permission/role if any
server-side invariant
```

Then verify:

- [ ] realm boundaries remain separate;
- [ ] server resolves authority from current DB state;
- [ ] client input does not grant authority;
- [ ] cross-provider/cross-realm access is tested;
- [ ] revocation/current state takes effect on the next request where required;
- [ ] legacy global RBAC is not introduced into new runtime code.

## 10.3 API contract workflow

When changing an endpoint:

- [ ] DTO validation is explicit;
- [ ] response contract is explicit;
- [ ] Swagger reflects the real response;
- [ ] derived ownership fields are not accepted from clients;
- [ ] stable error codes/localization are preserved;
- [ ] internal fields/PII do not leak;
- [ ] HTTP/full-flow tests cover the changed contract.

## 10.4 Business lifecycle workflow

Prefer explicit commands/transitions over arbitrary status patches.

For lifecycle changes, define:

```text
allowed source state
command
target state
actor/context
authorization
side effects
idempotency
invalid transition behavior
```

Test invalid transitions as well as the happy path.

---

# 11. Patterns to avoid

MUST NOT introduce these merely for convenience:

```text
module-root services/repositories/controllers directories
generic auth identity without a current use case
universal role engine
shared cross-platform role table
global Provider role identity
manual Provider ownership via customerId
manual migration timestamps
relative migration sequence numbers
runtime startup data migration
empty pass-through abstraction layers
circular dependencies hidden by barrels
trivial "file exists" tests
weakened assertions to satisfy CI
raw SQL sort/filter input
destructive reset of user work
```

Before creating a new abstraction, ask:

1. What invariant does it own?
2. What duplication or coupling does it remove?
3. Is there a real second consumer?
4. Does it make the business boundary clearer?

If the answers are weak, prefer the existing concrete implementation.

---

# 12. Verification gates

Run checks appropriate to the change. For a substantial PR, default to the full
pipeline:

```bash
npm ci
npm run build
npm run typecheck
npm run lint:check
npm test -- --no-coverage
npm run test:e2e -- --no-coverage
npm run migration:check-timestamps
npm run migration:verify
docker build --target production .
```

Rules:

- Migration commands are mandatory for schema changes.
- Relevant migration/integration tests are mandatory for migration behavior.
- Full-flow tests are mandatory for externally visible critical business
  behavior.
- Do not claim a command passed unless it was actually run.
- If a command cannot run because required infrastructure is unavailable,
  report exactly which command and why.

CI being green does not override an architecture or domain invariant violation.

---

# 13. Review priority

When tradeoffs exist, use this order:

```text
1. domain invariant
2. ownership boundary
3. authorization
4. API/data contract
5. migration/deployment safety
6. maintainability
7. observability
8. scale
9. optional abstraction
```

Do not trade a higher-priority invariant for a lower-priority convenience.

---

# 14. Completion report

Before saying the task is complete, report:

```text
Changed files:
- ...

Behavior/invariants changed:
- ...

Migrations:
- timestamp:
- owning feature:
- schema vs data:
- rollback:
- verification:

Tests/commands run:
- ...

Commands not run:
- ...

Remaining risks / follow-up:
- ...
```

Do not report "done" if a required check is still pending or known to fail.

---

# 15. Final checklist

Architecture:

- [ ] code is in the owning module and named feature;
- [ ] no module-root technical layer was introduced;
- [ ] dependency direction is explicit;
- [ ] no new circular dependency;
- [ ] no empty abstraction layer.

Authorization:

- [ ] realm semantics are explicit;
- [ ] Provider context is server validated;
- [ ] Provider supply ownership uses Provider ownership;
- [ ] legacy global RBAC was not reintroduced;
- [ ] cross-scope denial is tested when relevant.

Database:

- [ ] migration owner is correct;
- [ ] migration timestamp was generated with `date +%s%3N`;
- [ ] migration identity is unique and consistent;
- [ ] deployed migration history was not rewritten;
- [ ] schema/data migration distinction is correct;
- [ ] destructive changes follow expand/backfill/verify/contract;
- [ ] migration verification ran.

API:

- [ ] DTO validation is correct;
- [ ] Swagger matches the wire contract;
- [ ] ownership/internal fields do not leak;
- [ ] errors have stable localized messages.

Tests:

- [ ] changed behavior has behavioral coverage;
- [ ] fixed bugs have regression tests;
- [ ] full-flow critical workflow coverage exists;
- [ ] no trivial test was added solely for file presence;
- [ ] assertions were not weakened.

Delivery:

- [ ] build/typecheck/lint pass;
- [ ] relevant unit/full-flow/integration suites pass;
- [ ] migration gates pass when applicable;
- [ ] no unrelated user changes were destroyed;
- [ ] only Nexus Estate infrastructure was used.
